1. TERMS
   To avoid misunderstandings:
   1.1 The term "package", here, means any application installed in
       Slackware coming from the official repository or built with a
       slackbuild. 
   1.2 The term "module", here, means any extension enabled and build
       into the compilation of LAMMPS source to add methods or support
       to platforms that enhance the functionality of LAMMPS.
   This clarification is necessary because, in the LAMMPS documentation
   and source code, the term "package" refers to what we call "modules"
   here.

2. HOW TO DISABLE NOT REQUIRED MODULES 

Almost all modules that are supported by your system and dependencies
installed (autodetected) are enabled by default. You can disable those
not required in three different ways:
A. edit the code of the slackbuild 
   for example, a module enabled by default has this condition:
    atc="" [ "${ATC:-no}" = "yes" ] && atc="-DPKG_ATC=yes "
   if you want to disable it you have to edit as following:
    atc="" [ "${ATC:-no}" = "yes" ] && atc="-DPKG_ATC=yes "
B. remove the variables not required from the global $modules at line
   221. The script will not pass the related flags to cmake)
   for example, if you want to disable AMOEBA and ATC you have to 
   remove the strings $amoeba and $atc from modules=$amoeba$asphere...
C. pass to the script the related environment variable as following
    AMOEBA=no ATC=no ./lammps.SlackBuild

3. INTERDEPENDENT MODULES

   The enabling of some modules require that also other modules are
   enabled. For example, the ATC module requires also MANYBODY enabled.
   The script or cmake check if this condition is not respected, so all
   required additional modules will be enabled automatically
   
4. EXTERNAL MODULES

   Various modules are build as bundled features that interface LAMMPS
   to third part software that it should already installed in your
   system. These deps are released with the official Slackware (fftw or
   Eigen3 for example) or available in Slackbuilds.org. Some modules
   require the download of additional sources (listed in lammps.info).
   Please make sure to download all required extrafiles if you eenable
   the dependent module. If you do not do this, the process could fail
   or start the download during the compilation, although the script
   should prevent this checking the configuration of your system.
   
